se 3 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. Need to find a construction. Let's try to use one non-sunny line to cover many points, maybe vertical x=1 covering (1,1)-(1,4). Then we have remaining points: column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); column 3: (3,1),(3,2); column 4: (4,1). That's 6 points left, to be covered by 3 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 2-4 (3 columns), each line can cover at most 3 points. So 3 lines can cover at most 9 points, enough. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line's points are collinear with allowed slope.\n\nWe need to assign points to 3 lines. Let's try to assign each column's points to distinct lines.\n\nColumn 2 has 3 points, so each must go to a different line. Column 3 has 2 points, assign to two of the lines. Column 4 has 1 point, assign to any line.\n\nGoal: find lines that are sunny.\n\nLet's label lines L1, L2, L3.\n\nAssign column 2 points: (2,1) to L1, (2,2) to L2, (2,3) to L3.\n\nNow column 3 points: (3,1) and (3,2). Need to assign them to two lines, maybe L1 and L2, but need to check slopes.\n\nCheck L1 currently has (2,1). Adding (3,1) would be horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So cannot assign (3,1) to L1. Adding (3,2) to L1: slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So L1 can take (3,2). L2 currently has (2,2). Adding (3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. Adding (3,2): slope (2-2)/(3-2) = 0, not allowed. So L2 cannot take any point from column 3. L3 currently has (2,3). Adding (3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. Adding (3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So L3 can take (3,1). So assign (3,2) to L1, (3,1) to L3. L2 remains with only (2,2) for now.\n\nNow column 4 point (4,1). Need to assign to a line. Check each line:\n\n- L1 currently has (2,1) and (3,2). The line through these two points has slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, line y = x - 1? Actually (2,1) => 1 = 2 + b => b = -1, so line y = x - 1. At x=4, y = 3, not 1. So (4,1) not on L1.\n\n- L2 currently has (2,2)